interface.longDescription:
OneSignal is the customer messaging platform for push notifications, in-app messages, email, and SMS. This plugin brings OneSignal onboarding into Codex: point it at your codebase and it installs the SDK, sets up your push credentials, and proves that a real notification reaches a real device.

What the OneSignal plugin can do:
Detect your platform: Reads your project manifests to identify web (including Next.js, React, Vue, Angular, and Vite), iOS, Android, React Native, Expo, Flutter, Cordova, Capacitor, or Unity.
Install the SDK: Adds the OneSignal SDK with an exact, current version pin and the initialization code for your framework, in your existing code style and package manager.
Configure push credentials: Walks you through the Apple Developer and Firebase console steps, then uploads your APNs .p8 key and FCM service-account JSON to OneSignal for you. Guides email domain (SPF, DKIM, DMARC) and SMS sender setup.
Verify real delivery: Builds and runs your app, waits for the first device subscription to register, sends a test push, and confirms on the server that it was delivered.
Diagnose failures: Finds out why a notification does not arrive, such as missing credentials, a Site URL that does not match your origin, a service worker on the wrong origin, or a device that never subscribed.
Keep secrets out of your code: Reads API keys from environment variables, writes a gitignored .env, and scans its own changes for secrets before it finishes.
Show every change first: Declares the file list up front and shows the full diff for one approval before it writes.

Once installed, describe what you want in plain language or invoke the plugin directly with @OneSignal.

Try prompts like:
@OneSignal, set up push notifications in this Next.js app.
@OneSignal, add the OneSignal SDK to my Expo project.
@OneSignal, upload my APNs .p8 key and Firebase service account.
@OneSignal, why isn't my push notification arriving on Android?
@OneSignal, send a test push to my device and confirm delivery.
@OneSignal, check that my service worker is served from the right origin.

Requirements: A free OneSignal account and your App ID. A REST API key exported as ONESIGNAL_REST_API_KEY; the plugin never asks you to paste it into chat. Python 3.7 or newer on PATH for the helper scripts; if it is missing, the setup skill offers by-hand steps instead. A device, simulator, or browser that can receive the test push. Optional: sign in to the OneSignal MCP server so the plugin can look up users, check delivery, and send test messages as first-class tools.

Every edit happens on your machine through your local agent, with your approval. The plugin sends no source code, file paths, or credentials to OneSignal.
